Z||000000||Philphine||11-19-2001||07:01 AM||||<BR>something i wonder off and on.<P>knowing the weak rod story i sometimes wonder if any other cars that used the 2.5 that year had the same risk of fire and if the percentage of fire for other models is the same or even close (like if ?% of fieros caught on fire are the numbers similar for an '84 citation?).<P>if so, why did it stick with fiero and not other models? maybe because there were no engine options that year so the engine got tied exclusivly to the car? or it was the cars first year and it couldn't shake the reputation afterward (like what if it were the '86's that had the fire problem)?<P>just something i wonder about.<P>------------------<BR>Phil T.<P>start where you are,<BR>use what you have,<BR>do what you can.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Raydar||11-19-2001||07:16 AM||||I think the Fiero would have had more instances of fire because the catalytic converter is "front row, center" for any exploding engine events. Anything that poured out of the block would pour right on the converter. I've heard that under the right conditions, even antifreeze can burn. I've never seen it, though.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||hugh||11-19-2001||07:42 AM||||I don't think antifreeze as it is made now will burn,but years ago you could get antifreeze that was not permanent and was mostly alcohol.That would burn.||||reg||7
Z||000003||Nashco||11-19-2001||10:44 AM||||Antifreeze will most definitely burn!  Get your engine to running temperature and pour a gallon of it on the engine...I DARE YOU!  A couple years ago I had to replace a bunch of parts on a Cadillac that had a 500 ci big block that burst a coolant line and caught on fire.  Luckily it didn't screw up too much!<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000004||Steve Normington||11-19-2001||11:06 AM||||Also, people probably pushed a Fiero harder then they would an 84 Chevy Celebrity.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Formula88||11-19-2001||11:53 AM||||I've heard one of the big problems was two-fold.  One, Pontiac got a batch of bad connecting rods for the 84 engines.  These were recalled and replaced.  Two, the oiling system on the Fiero only holds 3 quarts of oil (2.5), so it's very susceptible to oil related failures.  Let it run a quart or two low, and you're gonna oil starve those marginal connecting rods.  Bingo - one busted connecting rod comes out the side of the block and dumps oil on the nearby catalytic converter.  Instant bonfire!<P>These issues have been taken care of in recalls, and are preventable with regular maintenance.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fformula88||11-19-2001||03:06 PM||||The low oil capacity is an issue, but from everything I have read it sounds like the faulty rods were the biggest problem.  After throwing a rod, oil would leak onth the manifold and down to the cat converter... and you quickly got a fire.  <P>I do not remember reading about this problem with other GM vehicles, and seem to recall it was specific to 2.5L's bound for Fiero's.  I cannot say for certain.<P>Lets also not forget, any car is caable of starting on fire if its not maintained.  Oil leaks can potentially get onto hot exhaust components and flame up (or coolant leaks!).<P>On the coolant, if its a true 50-50 mix, it isn't real flamable because its half water, but the lower the percentage of water, the more hazardous it becomes!||||reg||1
Z||000007||fldevel||11-19-2001||03:35 PM||||i had an 86 catch on fire due to a clogged cat wich made manafolds glow and the oil that was coming out of the valve cover hit the red hot manafold catching on fire and in turn caught the sparkplug whires,ecm whires etc on fire .. witch i blew out "im not a smoker  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">" i baught the car for $50 wich it had been sitting for 2 years and was driving it the 3 miles home ||||reg||1
